\title{Molien-Type Rigidity for Finite Group Actions:\\
Orbit Counts, Fixed-Point $q$-Series, and Effective Reconstruction}

\author{Aristotle}

\date{19 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Let $G$ be a finite group acting on a finite set $X$. Attached to this datum are the
fixed-point $q$-series $\Phi_{G,X}(q)=\sum_{g\in G}q^{|X^g|}$ and the orbit-counting
generating function $N_{G,X}(t)=\sum_{n\ge0}|X^{\times n}/G|\,t^{n}$, whose coefficients count
orbits of the diagonal action on $n$-tuples. We show that these determine one another, in a
sharp and effective form. Burnside's lemma applied to tuples identifies the $n$-th orbit count
with the $n$-th moment of the normalised fixed-point distribution
$\rho_{G,X}(v)=\#\{g:|X^g|=v\}/|G|$, a probability measure on $\{0,\dots,|X|\}$. Lagrange
interpolation then yields forward rigidity, a converse using only the orbit counts in degrees
$n\le\max(|X|,|Y|)$, and a dichotomy with no intermediate behaviour. We upgrade uniqueness to
computation: a universal rational matrix, the inverse of the moment (Vandermonde) matrix on the
nodes $0,1,\dots,N$ and depending on nothing but $N=|X|$, expresses $\rho_{G,X}$ as an explicit
linear functional of the first $N+1$ orbit counts; and a peeling recursion reads the densities
off one value at a time from the top down, with explicit geometric error $((m-1)/m)^n$ and a
rounding step that returns the fibre cardinalities exactly once
$((m-1)/m)^n\cdot 2|G|<1$. Finally we locate both boundaries: normalisation cannot be removed,
the number of coefficients cannot be reduced, and the invariants can never distinguish groups
(the regular actions of $\Zmod4$ and $\Zmod2\times\Zmod2$ agree in every degree), while for two
actions of one and the same group rigidity upgrades from densities to raw fixed-point multisets.
\end{abstract}

